    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1061">

                <pb facs="1191" n="1181" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94114 Supplies/7–1544: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the
                        Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-31T19:00:00-04:00">July 31, 1944—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1825. ReDepts 1708 and 1709 of July 15. You will note that the Japanese
                    Government is ready to transship mail as well as relief supplies. If the consent
                    of the Soviet Government is received to the conditions set forth in the Japanese
                    reply, you are requested to endeavor to obtain the Soviet Government’s
                    authorization for United States’ authorities immediately to load on Soviet
                    vessels bound for Vladivostok mail for prisoners of war and civilian internees
                    for transshipment on the Japanese vessel at Nakhodka.<anchor n="96"
                        xml:id="d1061-f1"/> This shipment of mail is meant to be supplementary to
                    and not to replace the regular mail routed via Teheran and Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1061-f1" n="96" xml:id="d1061-f1-content">
                <p>Ambassador Harriman reported in telegram 3200, August 28, 1944, that he had sent
                    a note asking that Soviet vessels from the United States west coast to
                    Vladivostok be authorized to carry mail for American prisoners of war and
                    civilian internees in Japan (711.94114 Supplies/8–2844).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
